<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="pyrimidine deoxyribonucleotides dephosphorylation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7206" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In addtion to their major role as precursors of DNA and RNA synthesis, nucleotides play various additional roles, such as signal transmitters, molecular switches, coenzymes and carriers of energy. for example, the pyrimidine ribonucleoside triphosphates |FRAME: CTP| and |FRAME: UTP| are utilized to form active intermediates in the biosynthesis of phospholipids and polysaccharides, respectively. Nucleotides are susceptible to various chemical modifications caused by endogenous reactive molecules such as reactive oxygen species, chemicals and ionizing irradiation. The incoporation of modified deoxynucleotides into DNA is hazardous and often leads to cell death. Nucleoside triphosphate diphosphatases (NTPases) are enzymes that hydrolyze the &amp;alpha;-&amp;beta; phosphodiester bond of nucleoside triphosphates (NTPs). The hydrolysis of this bond, often followed by hydrolysis of the released diphosphate, is a highly exergonic reaction that is often used in cell metabolism to ensure irreversibility of a particular reaction or a pathway. The action of NTPases is often used for the removal of non-canonical nucleotide triphosphates to prevent their incorporation into DNA or RNA or the degradation of a damaged nucleotide |CITS: [15740738]|. This collection of reactions includes three cases that are dedicated to the hydrolysis of pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates. The deoxynucleotide |FRAME: DUTP| is not a building block of DNA, and its incorporation in DNA is hazardous. The enzyme |FRAME: DUTP-PYROP-CPLX &quot;dUTP diphosphatase&quot;| (dUTPase) catalyzes the hydrolysis of dUTP to dUMP and diphosphate, acting to ensure chromosomal integrity by reducing the effective ratio of dUTP/dTTP |CITS: [8646528]|. The enzyme |FRAME: CPLX-8631 &quot;dCTP diphosphatase&quot;| has the highest activity with modified dCTP molecules such as |FRAME: CPD0-1344| and |FRAME: CPD-1094| |CITS: [19220460]|. It has been suggested that despite its activity with dCTP, its &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; substrates are modified analogs, and its role is the prevention of the incorporation of these analongs into DNA |CITS: [19220460]|. The enzyme |FRAME: G6954-MONOMER|, encoded in |FRAME: TAX-83333| by the |FRAME: G6954| gene, shows high specificity for hydrolysis of oxidized pyrimidine (deoxy)nucleoside triphosphates and is therefore expected to function in DNA damage avoidance |CITS: [11053429][11676470]|. Its preferred substrate appears to be |FRAME: 5-HYDROXY-CTP| |CITS: [12509230]|. A &lt;i&gt;nudG&lt;/i&gt; deletion strain exhibits a 2-3-fold elevated rate of |FRAME:HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE|-induced mutations |CITS: [14750949]|.</Description>
